25. Majority Element — LeetCode #169

Pattern: Boyer-Moore Voting Algorithm

class Solution {
    public int majorityElement(int[] nums) {

        int candidate = 0;
        int count = 0;

        for (int num : nums) {

            if (count == 0) {
                candidate = num;
            }

            if (num == candidate) {
                count++;
            } else {
                count--;
            }
        }

        return candidate;
    }
}

Example:

Input:  [2,2,1,1,1,2,2]
Output: 2
🧠 Logic

Majority element appears more than n/2 times.

Think like:

same number      → +1
different number → -1
count = 0        → choose new candidate

Because the majority element has more occurrences than all competing elements combined, it survives the cancellation.

Time: O(n)
Space: O(1)

26. Move Zeroes — LeetCode #283

Pattern: Two Pointers

class Solution {
    public void moveZeroes(int[] nums) {

        int index = 0;

        for (int num : nums) {

            if (num != 0) {
                nums[index] = num;
                index++;
            }
        }

        while (index < nums.length) {
            nums[index] = 0;
            index++;
        }
    }
}

Example:

Input:  [0,1,0,3,12]

Output: [1,3,12,0,0]
🧠 Logic

First collect all non-zero values:

[1,3,12,_,_]

Then fill remaining positions with 0:

[1,3,12,0,0]

Time: O(n)
Space: O(1)

27. Longest Consecutive Sequence — LeetCode #128

Pattern: HashSet

class Solution {
    public int longestConsecutive(int[] nums) {

        HashSet<Integer> set = new HashSet<>();

        for (int num : nums) {
            set.add(num);
        }

        int longest = 0;

        for (int num : set) {

            if (!set.contains(num - 1)) {

                int current = num;
                int length = 1;

                while (set.contains(current + 1)) {
                    current++;
                    length++;
                }

                longest = Math.max(longest, length);
            }
        }

        return longest;
    }
}

Example:

Input:  [100,4,200,1,3,2]

Output: 4

Because:

1 → 2 → 3 → 4

Length = 4.

🧠 Important trick

Why check:

if (!set.contains(num - 1))

?

Because num is the start of a sequence.

For:

1,2,3,4

Only 1 has no previous number.

So we start counting only from 1, avoiding unnecessary repeated work.

Time: O(n) average
Space: O(n)

